  "text": "**Review of \"The Great Gatsby\"**\n\nF. Scott Fitzgerald's *The Great Gatsby* is a timeless masterpiece that captures the spirit of the Roaring Twenties with vivid imagery and profound emotional depth. Set in the fictional city of West Egg, the novel follows the enigmatic Jay Gatsby, whose mysterious past and lavish parties mask a deep longing for the past\u2014a past embodied by Daisy Buchanan, the woman he once loved.\n\nGatsby\u2019s pursuit of Daisy is not just a romantic endeavor but a quest for the American Dream, a dream that is ultimately elusive and corrupt. The novel\u2019s setting is crucial; the distinction between West Egg and East Egg symbolizes the divide between old money and new money, highlighting the social class tensions that permeate the story.\n\nDaisy's character is complex, reflecting the superficiality and moral ambiguity of the upper class. Meanwhile, George Wilson, a working-class man, represents the innocence and simplicity that are gradually consumed by the greed and carelessness of the wealthy.\n\nFitzgerald\u2019s prose is lyrical and full of symbolism, from the green light at the end of Daisy\u2019s dock to the recurring motif of the eyes of Dr. T.J. Eckleburg, which loom over the valley of ashes\u2014a haunting reminder of the moral decay beneath the surface of the American Dream.\n\nThe novel\u2019s tragic ending serves as a powerful commentary on the futility of the American Dream and the destructive nature of obsession. It is a story that has resonated with readers for decades, not only for its narrative brilliance but also for its exploration of love, identity, and the illusions we create to justify our desires.\n\nIn conclusion, *The Great Gatsby* is a novel that lingers in the mind long after the final page. Its rich symbolism, unforgettable characters, and poignant themes make it a cornerstone of American literature.\n\n**Count of Proper Nouns from \"The Great Gatsby\":**  \n- West Egg  \n- Jay Gatsby  \n- Daisy Buchanan  \n- East Egg  \n- George Wilson  \n- Dr. T.J. Eckleburg  \n- The green light  \n- The valley of ashes  \n\n**Total: 8 proper nouns**",
